course: biology
lesson: 19
title: The tiger: the biggest and strongest cat, a solitary and cunning hunter
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The tiger is the biggest cat on Earth: a huge Amur or Bengal male can weigh about as much as three or four grown men and be about 3 metres long from nose to tail tip.
explain: Unlike the lion, the tiger lives and hunts alone, slipping through forest and tall grass, creeping close and low, then rushing its prey in one short burst.
explain: To us a tiger is bright orange, but deer cannot see red and orange well, so to them the striped tiger looks greenish and melts into the grass and shadows.
explain: Tigers surprise people: they love water and swim between islands, they say hello with a soft puffing chuff, and every tiger's stripes are as unique as your fingerprints.
explain: Only about 5,500 tigers are left in the wild, mostly in India, but with protection their numbers in India and Nepal have grown again.
fact: The tiger is the biggest of all cats; big Amur and Bengal males can weigh about 250 to 300 kilograms and be about 3 metres long with the tail, heavier than most lions.
fact: Every tiger has its own stripe pattern, and scientists use camera traps and stripes to recognise and count wild tigers; even the skin under the fur is striped.
fact: Stripes break up the tiger's outline, and deer cannot see red and orange well, so the orange tiger looks greenish to them, like grass.
fact: Tigers live and hunt alone, except mothers with cubs, and each tiger needs a big area of its own because its prey is spread out thinly; cubs stay with their mother for about 2 years, learning to hunt.
fact: Tigers stalk slowly and quietly, then make a short rush from close by, because they are strong but not built for long chases; perhaps only about 1 hunt in 10 succeeds.
fact: Tigers love water and swim well; Sundarbans tigers swim between islands in the mangroves of India and Bangladesh and can swim several kilometres.
fact: Tigers usually attack from behind or the side, so Sundarbans honey collectors once wore face masks on the backs of their heads to make a tiger think it was being watched.
fact: Tigers greet friends with a soft puffing sound called chuffing, or prusten; they can roar too, but chuffing is for friends.
fact: Tigers have white spots on the backs of their ears, which may help cubs follow their mother or look like eyes watching; servals and many other wild cats have them too.
fact: A mirror layer at the back of a tiger's eye bounces light back through it, so tigers see far better than us at night and their eyes shine in headlights.
fact: There are only about 5,500 wild tigers, mostly in India; they lost most of their forests to farms and were hunted for skins and bones, but numbers in India and Nepal have grown again thanks to protection.
fact: A liger has a lion father and a tiger mother; ligers are bred only in zoos and parks, because lions and tigers live in different places in the wild, and ligers grow bigger than both parents.
exercise: Which is the biggest cat in the world? | The tiger | The lion | The jaguar | The snow leopard
exercise: How much can a big Amur or Bengal tiger weigh? | About 250 to 300 kilograms | About 25 to 30 kilograms, like a big dog | About 2 to 3 tonnes | About 100 grams
exercise: How do scientists recognise each wild tiger? | By its own stripe pattern, photographed by camera traps | By the colour of its eyes, looked at through strong binoculars | By the sound of its roar | By the length of its tail
exercise: What is under a tiger's striped fur? | Striped skin | Plain white skin | Plain orange skin | Spotted skin like a leopard
exercise: Why does a bright orange tiger hide well from deer? | Deer cannot see orange well, so it looks greenish like grass | Deer are colour blind to black stripes only | The tiger changes colour like a chameleon to match the grass around it | Deer only look at the sky
exercise: How do tigers usually live? | Alone, except mothers with cubs | In big prides of females, like lions | In pairs for life | In herds with deer
exercise: How long do tiger cubs stay with their mother? | About 2 years | About 2 weeks | About 20 years | They leave on the day they are born
exercise: How does a tiger hunt? | It creeps close, then makes a short rush | It chases prey for many kilometres | It digs traps in the ground | It waits for the prey to fall asleep and walks up
exercise: About how many tiger hunts succeed? | Perhaps 1 in 10 | Every single one | 9 out of 10 | 1 in 1,000
exercise: How do tigers feel about water? | They love it, swim well and bathe to cool off | They hate it, like most pet cats, and keep well away | They drown if they fall in | They only drink it, never enter it
exercise: Why did Sundarbans honey collectors wear masks on the backs of their heads? | Tigers attack from behind, so a face behind made them think they were watched | To keep bees off their necks | To look scary to crocodiles | To keep their hair and necks dry in the heavy monsoon rain
exercise: What does a tiger's soft puffing chuff mean? | Hello, I mean no harm | Go away or I will attack | I am hungry | I am lost
exercise: What may the white spots on the backs of a tiger's ears do? | Help cubs follow their mother or look like eyes watching | Help it hear better | Reflect sunlight to keep it cool | Show other tigers how old it is and where it was born
exercise: Why do a tiger's eyes shine in car headlights? | A mirror layer at the back of the eye bounces light back | They make their own light, like a torch, to see in the dark | They are wet with tears | They are made of glass
exercise: About how many tigers live in the wild today? | About 5,500 | About 55 | About 5 million | About 550,000
exercise: Where do most wild tigers live? | India | Africa | Australia | South America
exercise: What is a liger? | A cross with a lion father and a tiger mother, bred only in zoos | A wild cat of the African jungle | A small striped cat of the mountains | A baby tiger before its stripes grow
test: My mate says lions are the biggest cats since they're the kings. right? | No, the tiger is the biggest cat, heavier than most lions | Yes, lions are much bigger and heavier than tigers, by a long way | Yes, lions weigh a tonne | No, the cheetah is biggest
test: In a cartoon, a pack of tigers hunts together like wolves. What is wrong? | Tigers live and hunt alone, except mothers with cubs | Nothing, tigers always hunt in big packs, just like wolves do | Tigers hunt only in water | Tigers do not hunt at all
test: In a game, a tiger refuses to cross a river because cats hate water. What would a real tiger do? | Swim across, tigers love water | Wait for a bridge | Jump over the whole river | Turn back and never cross
test: If a deer could see colours like we do, what would happen to the tiger? | It would spot the orange tiger more easily | Nothing, the tiger would still look green | The tiger would turn green | The deer would stop eating grass
test: A tiger fails its first 9 hunts this week. Is something wrong with it? | Not necessarily, perhaps only 1 hunt in 10 succeeds | Yes, real tigers never miss | Yes, it must be blind | No, tigers do not really need to eat every week
test: 2 tigers at the zoo, how many white ear spots can i count if i see both backs of their heads | 4 | 2 | 8 | 1
test: A tiger mum with 3 cubs. How many legs and how many tails? | 16 legs and 4 tails | 12 legs and 4 tails | 16 legs and 1 tail | 8 legs and 4 tails
test: Why would two tigers living in one small patch of forest have trouble? | Prey is spread out, so there is only enough for one tiger in each area | Tigers are scared of each other's stripes, so they keep running away | They would get too warm together | Two tigers always become friends
test: A keeper hears a tiger make a soft puffing sound. Should she worry? | No, chuffing is a friendly hello | Yes, it means it will attack | Yes, it means it is ill | No, it means it is tired and wants to sleep now
test: Ali reads that a liger is bigger than a lion and a tiger. Where might he see one? | Only in a zoo or park, never in the wild | In the forests of India | On the African savanna | Deep in the Sundarbans mangroves
test: ugh tigers are basically extinct anyway, why bother | No, about 5,500 remain, and protection has made numbers grow in India and Nepal | Yes, there are none left anywhere at all, not even in the forests of India | Yes, only cartoon tigers exist | No, there are millions in Africa
test: Why do scientists put camera traps in the forest instead of catching tigers? | Each tiger's stripes are unique, so photos are enough to count them | Tigers like having their photos taken | Cameras keep tigers away from villages, so the forest stays safe for people | Catching tigers is easy but boring
exam: Identify the largest living member of the cat family. | The tiger | The lion | The jaguar | The leopard
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the social habits of the tiger? | It lives and hunts alone, mothers accompanied by their cubs excepted | It hunts invariably in packs of considerable size, after the manner of the wolf | It lives in prides governed by females | It forms permanent herds with its prey
exam: Ascertain the principal reason for which the orange coat of the tiger affords concealment from deer. | Deer are unable to perceive red and orange well, the tiger appearing greenish | Deer perceive exclusively in black and white at all times | The coat of the tiger alters its colour in accordance with the season | Deer are incapable of perceiving movement
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the present population of wild tigers. | Approximately five thousand five hundred, the majority in India | Approximately fifty-five, all in captivity | Several million, distributed across Africa | None whatsoever, the species having been wholly extinct in the wild for a century
